WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.080
Today we are going to take a bunch of celebrities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:15.200
and imagine what they looked as tiny little&nbsp;
babies. And then, we will also make them old. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.080
at the end of this video, I’ll also step&nbsp;
up to the plate, and become a baby myself.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.880
So, what is this black magic here? Well, what&nbsp;
you see here is a bunch of synthetic humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:33.280
created by a learning-based technique called&nbsp;
StyleGAN3, which appeared this year, in June&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.840
2021. It is a neural network-based learning&nbsp;
algorithm that is capable of synthesizing these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.960
eye-poppingly detailed images of human beings&nbsp;
that don’t even exist, and even animate them.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:55.360
Now, how does it do all this black magic? Well,&nbsp;
it takes walks in a latent space. What is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.760
A latent space is a made-up place&nbsp;
where we are trying to organize data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:06.000
in a way that similar things are close to each&nbsp;
other. In our earlier work, we were looking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.240
generate hundreds of variants of a material model&nbsp;
to populate this scene. In this latent space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.640
we can concoct all of these really&nbsp;
cool digital material models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:19.840
A link to this work is available&nbsp;
in the video description.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.840
StyleGAN uses walks in a similar latent space&nbsp;
to create these human faces and animate them.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.200
And now, hold on to your papers, because a&nbsp;
latent space can represent not only materials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:40.880
or the head movement and smiles for people, but&nbsp;
even better, age too. You remember these amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:47.200
transformations from the intro. So, how does it&nbsp;
do that? Well, similarly to the font and material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:54.640
examples, we can embed the source image into a&nbsp;
latent space, and take a path therein. It looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.080
like this. Please remember this embedding step,&nbsp;
because we are going to refer to it in a moment.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.640
And now comes the twist, the latent space for&nbsp;
this new method is built such that when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:13.840
take these walks, it disentangles age from other&nbsp;
attributes. This means that only the age changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.520
and nothing else changes. This is very&nbsp;
challenging to pull off, because normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.560
when we change our location in the latent space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.840
not just one thing changes, everything&nbsp;
changes. This was the case with the materials.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.120
But not with this method, which can&nbsp;
take photos of well-known celebrities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:39.760
and make them look younger or older. I&nbsp;
kind of want to do this myself too. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:46.720
you know what? Now, it’s my turn. This is what&nbsp;
baby Károly might look like after reading baby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:53.120
papers. And this is old man Károly, complaining&nbsp;
that papers were way better back in his day.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.840
And this is supposedly baby Károly from a talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.040
a NATO conference. Look, apparently&nbsp;
they let anybody in these days!

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.640
Now, this is all well and good, but there&nbsp;
is a price to be paid for all this. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.800
what is the price? Let’s find out together&nbsp;
what that is. Here is the reference image of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.680
me. And here is how the transformations came&nbsp;
out. Did you find the issue? Well, the issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.680
is that I don’t really look like this. Not only&nbsp;
because this beard was synthesized onto my face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:35.360
by an earlier AI, but really, I can’t really find&nbsp;
my exact image in this. Take another look. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.880
is what the input image looked like. Can you&nbsp;
find it in the outputs somewhere? Not really.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.840
Same with the conference image, this&nbsp;
is the actual original image of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.560
and this is the output of the AI.&nbsp;
So, I can’t find myself. Why is that?

00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:00.000
Now, you remember I mentioned earlier that we&nbsp;
embed the source image into the latent space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.400
And this step, is, unfortunately, imperfect.&nbsp;
We start out from not exactly the same image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:12.960
but only something similar to it. This is the&nbsp;
price to be paid for these amazing results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.440
and with that, please remember to&nbsp;
invoke the First Law of Papers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.200
Which says, do not look at where we are, look at&nbsp;
